WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:09.250
Ashley: If I asked all of you to tell me the
first thing that comes to mind when I say

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:13.590
the word play, there'd probably be as many
answers as there are people in this audience.

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:19.070
For a topic that seems so familiar and universal,
it's oddly elusive and hard to nail down in

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:25.130
words. To some it may connote running around
outside in the sun, a childhood memory, a

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:31.680
favorite game, toy or gadget. The varieties
of play are vast and unique to age and culture.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:36.550
In today's high tech world the proliferation
of wireless devices, alternate reality games

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:39.671
and social media have only further broadened
the landscape of play.

00:00:39.671 --> 00:00:45.600
Tonight's talk is part of a larger series
called "Designing Play" which began in February,

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:50.979
and invites people of all ages to invent,
explore, tinker, adventure, and have fun.

00:00:50.979 --> 00:00:55.769
Over the past few months, we've relied on
designers of all types, graphic, landscape,

00:00:55.769 --> 00:00:59.750
and architecturally trained ones, to create
hands-on opportunities for our visitors to

00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:06.120
play and problem solve. The result: unbridled
creativity and innovation that's given rise

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:11.360
to structures built from toothpicks and gumdrops,
portraits construed from various styles of

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:15.100
typeface, and landscape drawings that allow
the imagination to run wild.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:20.600
I've been very excited for this evening's
talk, which is the culminating moment of this

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:25.300
series. Tonight's guest, Edith Ackermann,
is what I might call a play expert. Trained

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:30.150
as a developmental psychologist, she received
her Doctor of Developmental Psychology, two

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:34.670
Master's Degrees in Developmental and Clinical
Psychology, and a Bachelor of Experimental

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:39.860
Psychology, all from the University of Geneva,
Switzerland, and is currently a visiting scientist

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:46.191
at Massachusetts Institute of Technology,
School of Architecture. Her research had led

00:01:46.191 --> 00:01:51.770
her to team up with partners such as MIT Media
Lab, The Lego Learning Institute, and the

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:56.550
Exploratory Museum in San Francisco, to help
shape the future of play and learning in the

00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:57.820
digital world.

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:02.370
She comes from a lineage of thinkers and scientists
who have transformed the fields of child development

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:09.269
and education, including John Piaget, who
she studied and collaborated with, Maria Montessori,

00:02:09.269 --> 00:02:15.140
Leva Gatski, and Seymour Papert, all of whom
strongly believed that learners construct

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:17.170
knowledge through play.

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:21.870
Ackermann explains the overall purpose of
her work as three-fold: to bring users back

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:27.710
into the design process, to imagine and design
activities, artifacts, and spaces that bring

00:02:27.710 --> 00:02:33.590
about delight and foster personal and societal
growth, and to help innovative teams of researchers

00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:38.790
and practitioners bring strategic research
design and a user centered design approach

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:40.560
to product development.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:44.730
I'd like to quote Edith, who says there are
two lessons that she has learned through her

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:50.569
career in research. When it comes to learning
and creative uses of technologies, children

00:02:50.569 --> 00:02:56.100
have more to teach adults than adults to children.
When it comes to innovating for others, don't

00:02:56.100 --> 00:03:01.110
guess what they want or do what they say,
co- create what they and you will love once

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:02.160
it is there.

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:06.310
I'd like to welcome Edith Ackermann.

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:08.970
[applause]

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:24.020
Edith Ackermann: Thank you, Ashley, and thank
you for having me come to the Walker Arts

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:30.600
Center. I am coming more from the sciences
and I am moving towards the arts, so it's

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:38.599
a great pleasure for me to be able to talk
here at the Walker Arts Center. The title

00:03:38.599 --> 00:03:49.239
of my talk is "Playful Inventions and Explorations,"
which I borrowed, actually, from a title of

00:03:49.239 --> 00:03:58.050
an institute and the network that exists and
that grew out of work that had been done at

00:03:58.050 --> 00:04:07.140
the MIT Media Lab to begin with, in the group
that was lead by Mitchel Reznick, Seymour

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:18.470
Papert, and other people. And then it grew
across the country and it included, educators,

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:27.810
researchers, artists, practitioners, politicians,
and the purpose was actually to engage children

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:40.430
in activities that are innovative, that are
playful. There is a whole evolution of this

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:50.410
work that is happening also here in Minneapolis,
in this science museum, and the Exploratorium,

00:04:50.410 --> 00:04:53.790
where I had the chance to work quite a bit.

00:04:53.790 --> 00:05:07.350
So, this evening, I would like to talk a little
bit about these relationships between play,

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:17.350
imagination, creativity, innovation, and design.
As Ashley said, there are as many definitions

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:24.130
of play as people in this room. And it has
been very difficult for me to prepare this

00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:30.440
talk, because each time when you enter in
the domain you lose yourself, you get lost.

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:37.790
So, it's just my take on the question tonight,
and it keeps changing and I just want to,

00:05:37.790 --> 00:05:40.440
in a way, almost introduce a discussion.

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:49.020
So, I will talk about children's learning,
children's play, play and creativity in children

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:55.889
and grown-ups, play for education, which is
a difficult topic, its like, "What could it

00:05:55.889 --> 00:06:08.240
be?" The future of play in children, so-called
degenerates. And last but not least, if I

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:15.169
ever get there, is to think about places and
environments that can actually foster the

00:06:15.169 --> 00:06:21.050
type of learning and play I want to talk about.

00:06:21.050 --> 00:06:33.740
So, let me start by talking a little bit about
the strong beliefs, and it's more that beliefs

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:39.960
that actually--some of the lessons that I
learned in working, especially with Jean Piaget,

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:50.301
but then with other people on what learning
is about. So, the idea is that children may

00:06:50.301 --> 00:06:56.820
not have much experience or knowledge, and
I mean experience in the sense of--in German

00:06:56.820 --> 00:07:02.220
you have these beautiful distinctions that
don't exist in other languages--erfahrung

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:09.970
means experience as you are old and wise,
and erlebneiss is experience as being totally,

00:07:09.970 --> 00:07:16.320
mindfully into situations. So, the children
may not have much experience in the sense

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:25.260
erfahrung or knowledge, but are sure born
with the knack to do the right thing in order

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:34.889
to find out about what they don't know yet.
This is an important point. One way of talking

00:07:34.889 --> 00:07:39.520
about this, is to say they are experts in
self-directed learning, what ever that means.

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:49.449
I like to think of a child that is busy figuring
out their place in the world as a traveler

00:07:49.449 --> 00:07:59.759
or newcomer to a foreign land that has to
convey their needs that have to be heard without

00:07:59.759 --> 00:08:04.330
really speaking the language, without knowing
the habits, that have to plow along without

00:08:04.330 --> 00:08:12.130
knowing the [unintelligible 8:05] very well,
deciding when to move on and when to settle,

00:08:12.130 --> 00:08:20.420
deciding what to bring along and what to leave
behind, and deciding who to trust and not

00:08:20.420 --> 00:08:21.420
to trust.

00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:25.910
In a way, we have all had this experience
when we travel, I think. This is why people

00:08:25.910 --> 00:08:33.819
like to travel, because it's like enacting
and re-experiencing these very fundamental

00:08:33.819 --> 00:08:45.060
ways of handling uncertain situations. And
the point here is that is what impressive

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:51.890
with children is that it's not just that they
are very good at languages. We know that.

00:08:51.890 --> 00:08:57.870
It's just the tip of the iceberg. It is that
they are incredible and we can learn a lot

00:08:57.870 --> 00:09:04.660
from them in the way they maneuver and evolve
in a world that is by definition too complex,

00:09:04.660 --> 00:09:08.630
uncertain, and unpredictable for them.

00:09:08.630 --> 00:09:16.750
So, that's the starting point. I do like the
way in which Jean Piaget, who was my mentor,

00:09:16.750 --> 00:09:24.510
and a Swiss developmental psychologist who
is quite well known. He has had a strong influence

00:09:24.510 --> 00:09:30.160
in education, even if he didn't think of himself
as an educator. And that's another interesting

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:31.160
point.

00:09:31.160 --> 00:09:40.210
He defined intelligence as adaptation. What
he said is that to adapt is not just to accommodate,

00:09:40.210 --> 00:09:47.180
as we always think of it. It is not to change
ones views, or one's ways of being, or manners,

00:09:47.180 --> 00:09:56.510
or even one's look just to conform to one's
surroundings. Like a chameleon who uses the

00:09:56.510 --> 00:10:06.200
camouflage technique of changing color to
disappear in the background, or the character

00:10:06.200 --> 00:10:14.790
of Zelligen in Budiiain's film, who always
tries to please whoever is around them, tries

00:10:14.790 --> 00:10:25.820
to be loved and belong so strongly that he
has no spinal cord anymore. He's like a chameleon.

00:10:25.820 --> 00:10:32.990
So, if you think of the ways in which people
adapt to their world, and especially young

00:10:32.990 --> 00:10:39.019
people who are still very malleable because
they still have still a lot to learn, one

00:10:39.019 --> 00:10:48.209
important aspect that sometimes gets underestimated
is that you also have to stay whole. You have

00:10:48.209 --> 00:10:56.550
to withstand; you have to remain who you are.
You have to keep your identity, even if you

00:10:56.550 --> 00:11:06.050
are constantly put into situations that require,
in a way, that you change your views.

00:11:06.050 --> 00:11:14.350
What is very strong is this notion that to
adapt is not to control the uncontrollable

00:11:14.350 --> 00:11:24.230
or to predict what is unpredictable. But it
is in a way this notion that you have to create

00:11:24.230 --> 00:11:35.670
a niche in which it becomes possible to actually
live and evolve in ways that are pleasant

00:11:35.670 --> 00:11:43.810
and agreeable to the person, organism that
we are considering.

00:11:43.810 --> 00:11:53.720
The whole theory of Piaget that I am very
influenced by is built around this notion

00:11:53.720 --> 00:12:01.550
of what it means to actually be fulfilled
personally, and also intellectually, and in

00:12:01.550 --> 00:12:09.030
a way to succeed in the world. So, at the
beginning, the children are very fused with

00:12:09.030 --> 00:12:14.490
their world. There are no distinctions between
themselves and the world. And as they grow,

00:12:14.490 --> 00:12:21.070
there is this bifurcation. But they always
happen at the same time. It's like socialization,

00:12:21.070 --> 00:12:28.890
individuation. Becoming an agent and becoming
in a way, able to relate to other people.

00:12:28.890 --> 00:12:31.450
Attachment, detachment.

00:12:31.450 --> 00:12:40.280
And as they grow older, and I will not focus
so much on that, the experiential field of

00:12:40.280 --> 00:12:47.190
a person is expanding. They become able to
not just act in the here and now, but they

00:12:47.190 --> 00:12:53.630
draw lessons from the past to project into
the future and vice-versa. And also the field,

00:12:53.630 --> 00:13:01.740
as in the space, is expanding. People move
about as they grow older and expand their

00:13:01.740 --> 00:13:11.800
field. Their maneuvering techniques and their
belief systems become more stable and more

00:13:11.800 --> 00:13:12.959
mobile.

00:13:12.959 --> 00:13:23.459
So, what the children--we know they are learning
everywhere, and all the time, in school, out

00:13:23.459 --> 00:13:31.480
of school. They are learning through play
which leads grown-ups to say that play is

00:13:31.480 --> 00:13:42.800
the child's more serious work. Now. what I
want to try to address is what does that mean,

00:13:42.800 --> 00:13:50.310
what are the implications for anyone who is
interested in designing environments for young

00:13:50.310 --> 00:13:59.459
people to actually grow, and live, and learn.

00:13:59.459 --> 00:14:05.399
So, I will talk a little bit more about Jean
Piaget, then I will let go of him. He is mostly

00:14:05.399 --> 00:14:11.710
known for his stages, developmental stages.
It is important because it provides a window

00:14:11.710 --> 00:14:20.060
in what young people are interested in and
able to do, generally at different levels

00:14:20.060 --> 00:14:21.420
or ages in their development.

00:14:21.420 --> 00:14:31.150
There is more to Piaget than these stages,
and the part that interests me the most is

00:14:31.150 --> 00:14:44.350
this notion that children have very coherent
and robust belief systems, and that their

00:14:44.350 --> 00:14:55.730
belief systems are stubborn. They not easy
to shake, and this is so for very good adaptive

00:14:55.730 --> 00:15:06.360
reasons. The whole theory shows that--it's
not to say that the children don't change

00:15:06.360 --> 00:15:11.779
or are not influenced when they relate to
others, or when they interact with the world.

00:15:11.779 --> 00:15:19.990
Their views are constantly evolving. But they
are evolving according to almost a logic of

00:15:19.990 --> 00:15:24.980
their own.

00:15:24.980 --> 00:15:34.339
When their belief system changes, it is jump-wise.
What this means is that it's not just because

00:15:34.339 --> 00:15:40.910
you experience something at some time that
you are going to learn these pieces of knowledge.

00:15:40.910 --> 00:15:52.430
You act in the world in different ways and
you begin to form a very structured belief

00:15:52.430 --> 00:15:59.170
system. He calls it "structure," but it doesn't
matter. These pieces of knowledge are ways

00:15:59.170 --> 00:16:07.090
of making sense of the world. They hang together.
They have co-dependencies between one another.

00:16:07.090 --> 00:16:14.320
All this is to say that this has profound
implications for education. The first profound

00:16:14.320 --> 00:16:20.459
implication for education of this view is
that teaching is always indirect. No matter

00:16:20.459 --> 00:16:28.040
whether we like it or not as educators, teaching
is always indirect because people will always

00:16:28.040 --> 00:16:37.100
interpret, assimilate what we tell them, teach
them, show them, the way we guide them into

00:16:37.100 --> 00:16:47.040
situations according to their own belief systems,
experiences. They will re-translate it.

00:16:47.040 --> 00:16:56.610
The more disturbing and important consequence
is that--Piaget used to say it in a very provocative

00:16:56.610 --> 00:17:01.690
way--he said, "Whatever you teach to a learner,
you don't allow them to discover by themselves."

00:17:01.690 --> 00:17:07.720
It's this notion of "Wait, wait, don't tell
me." When kids say, "Wait, wait, don't tell

00:17:07.720 --> 00:17:14.159
me," it's "I want to discover it myself. I
need leads, I need offerings, but I want to

00:17:14.159 --> 00:17:19.750
discover it by myself." And related is this
notion that any theory of learning that ignores

00:17:19.750 --> 00:17:27.000
our present paying of attention to resistances
to learning or teaching in a way misses the

00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:28.350
point.

00:17:28.350 --> 00:17:34.070
Another important idea was developed more
by people I worked with later in my life,

00:17:34.070 --> 00:17:39.580
so I've spent sort of half of my life trying
to understand how children's worldviews evolve

00:17:39.580 --> 00:17:49.259
in different domains, mostly in the sciences
at the time. Then I started working when I

00:17:49.259 --> 00:17:58.700
came to the US with designers, educators,
people who actually tried to design environments

00:17:58.700 --> 00:18:04.419
for these kinds of learning to happen.

00:18:04.419 --> 00:18:12.070
One of the important ideas, also from the
background working with Piaget and the whole

00:18:12.070 --> 00:18:19.440
team, is that there are all these paradoxes.
If you actually want children to internalize

00:18:19.440 --> 00:18:27.399
their action or to become a good thinker,
a good way of doing it is to let them externalize

00:18:27.399 --> 00:18:35.830
what they think, because if you project out
or if you build something based on--or if

00:18:35.830 --> 00:18:42.330
you re-enact something based on ideas that
you have, you're entering a dialogue with

00:18:42.330 --> 00:18:47.179
your own expressions and enactments. And it
allows you to progress.

00:18:47.179 --> 00:18:52.379
So, it's again a paradox, and there are many
examples. I don't have the time to go into

00:18:52.379 --> 00:18:59.200
it, but if you are really interested in getting
kids to get the sense of what, for example,

00:18:59.200 --> 00:19:06.330
numbers are about or understanding the concept
of numerosity, or number three, there are

00:19:06.330 --> 00:19:11.879
plenty of ways that you can show embodiments
of that phenomenon in different contexts.

00:19:11.879 --> 00:19:15.269
You contextualize them differently.

00:19:15.269 --> 00:19:21.750
It's never just one, you see. It's not like
we're going to now talk about fractions, and

00:19:21.750 --> 00:19:30.080
we are going to do the pi's. That doesn't
work. You need the whole array of possible

00:19:30.080 --> 00:19:35.770
embodiments of the concept, and it's the learner
who makes the connections between when they

00:19:35.770 --> 00:19:40.779
are ready, between these different contexts.

00:19:40.779 --> 00:19:56.350
I now want to try to talk more about play.
I have spoken about learning too long. I like

00:19:56.350 --> 00:20:04.850
to think of play as the art of world making,
and that play is about inventing invented

00:20:04.850 --> 00:20:14.790
realities. It is about creating a world, physical
or virtual, inhabiting that world, and then

00:20:14.790 --> 00:20:17.989
eventually becoming inhabited by it.

00:20:17.989 --> 00:20:27.440
Now, sometimes you are invited into invented
realities that have been made or designed

00:20:27.440 --> 00:20:36.080
by other people. I like to start with very
silly examples, like the entertainment park.

00:20:36.080 --> 00:20:44.489
I think this is the word where the roller
coasters are. Imagine humanity has invented

00:20:44.489 --> 00:20:52.539
for itself these crazy devices where people
actually go there to either take the driver's

00:20:52.539 --> 00:21:02.399
seat and sort of experience the thrills of
things that if they did it in the real world,

00:21:02.399 --> 00:21:11.460
it would be really dangerous. [laughter] Scary.
Or be taken for a ride, even, be taken for

00:21:11.460 --> 00:21:20.419
a ride where you experience things in ways
that are just not so usual in real life.

00:21:20.419 --> 00:21:26.099
In a way, I like to take the example of the
entertainment park because we are all thinking

00:21:26.099 --> 00:21:31.899
that this is not where you play. But in a
way it is very interesting to think about

00:21:31.899 --> 00:21:38.139
why humanity has invented these kinds of things.

00:21:38.139 --> 00:21:45.649
The metaphor of leaping is often used. I have
a new material to leap, to capture the sense

00:21:45.649 --> 00:21:52.899
of exuberance and freedom that characterizes
children's play this time, and I think it's

00:21:52.899 --> 00:21:58.739
a good metaphor. People leap when they feel
like breaking loose or shaking off their habitual

00:21:58.739 --> 00:22:07.440
ways of doing things, and of thinking. They
want to move out of their comfort zone.

00:22:07.440 --> 00:22:15.070
So, we leap, for example, when we look at
things from different and surprising angles.

00:22:15.070 --> 00:22:21.850
When we zoom in and out of a situation, as
like when you look at the drop of milk through

00:22:21.850 --> 00:22:29.679
a microscope, or if you hover over Istanbul
and the south of France via Google Earth,

00:22:29.679 --> 00:22:36.590
when you actually sit in an airplane and you
see the landscape becoming smaller. Each time

00:22:36.590 --> 00:22:41.200
I am amazed by this.

00:22:41.200 --> 00:22:48.070
We leap when we cross borders, when we transgress,
when we take a walk on the wild side, as Louis

00:22:48.070 --> 00:22:56.450
was saying. And in our minds, we leap when
we mentally move between reelas that wouldn't

00:22:56.450 --> 00:23:08.190
otherwise be juxtaposed in our everyday experience
that are unlikely, or when juxtaposing these

00:23:08.190 --> 00:23:15.599
worlds, worlds that are not normally traversed.
You bring them together. So, that's a more

00:23:15.599 --> 00:23:19.600
mental way of thinking about what leaping
is.

00:23:19.600 --> 00:23:27.519
An example is when you move backwards and
forward in time which children always do in

00:23:27.519 --> 00:23:32.769
pretend play. I am the baby. I am the poppa.
I am the momma. They like the Russian dolls

00:23:32.769 --> 00:23:40.180
because it's a spatial version of it, almost.
The little one goes in the big one, and then

00:23:40.180 --> 00:23:46.840
you put them in a row. All these games. It's
talking to our dolls, to imaginary companions.

00:23:46.840 --> 00:23:55.330
It's a displacement pretense that all the
pretends play. Putting one's self in other

00:23:55.330 --> 00:23:59.600
people's shoes. it's more role play.

00:23:59.600 --> 00:24:06.399
Now, one can go a long way using the metaphor
of the leap to think about play, but I feel,

00:24:06.399 --> 00:24:14.340
and this is a more personal add-on, I think
that the metaphor of the leap is important.

00:24:14.340 --> 00:24:20.269
But what is equally important to think about
when we think about play is what I call the

00:24:20.269 --> 00:24:31.719
"come back". It's a sense of sort of a settling
exhilaration that young children can feel

00:24:31.719 --> 00:24:39.820
that emerge through enacting, in a way of
return.

00:24:39.820 --> 00:24:46.820
So, the reason why this is important is that
you cannot leap without landing, or there

00:24:46.820 --> 00:24:53.649
is no levity without gravity. So, as much
as kids love to move on--imagine a toddler,

00:24:53.649 --> 00:25:04.039
the thrills of all of a sudden being able
to travel in the world and to have this whole

00:25:04.039 --> 00:25:10.899
world turning around them while they move
on, they also like to come back.

00:25:10.899 --> 00:25:16.930
The whole toy industry for young children
is built around this notion that, for example,

00:25:16.930 --> 00:25:23.991
when a young child moves about in the world
and is disoriented, there is a reason to have

00:25:23.991 --> 00:25:30.220
a pull along toy with you, because what it
does is that it gives you a sense that there

00:25:30.220 --> 00:25:35.990
is something invariant. There is something
constant. It's your distance from it. It's

00:25:35.990 --> 00:25:43.169
the fact that it follows you, and it creates
the sense almost of a very ephemeral home

00:25:43.169 --> 00:25:49.379
for a little while, which is important.

00:25:49.379 --> 00:25:56.230
The children play home. One of our colleagues,
Diane Willow, actually just put a tent inside

00:25:56.230 --> 00:26:03.510
of the Children's Museum with light coming
from inside it. It's a beautiful exhibit because

00:26:03.510 --> 00:26:13.940
it taps into children's desire to have tree
houses, their own little secret gardens and

00:26:13.940 --> 00:26:14.940
so on.

00:26:14.940 --> 00:26:22.200
There is another thing that is important.
It's the Hansel and Gretel aspect. It's nice

00:26:22.200 --> 00:26:30.799
to wander into the forest. It's nice to go
wait for the moment of magic. It's nice to

00:26:30.799 --> 00:26:37.460
go catch the butterflies, but it's also nice
to know that you can come back. And the whole

00:26:37.460 --> 00:26:44.149
mythology of the labyrinth, of leaving a thread
behind, of leaving bread crumbs behind, is

00:26:44.149 --> 00:26:50.899
an enactment of this desire to both move out,
transgress, walk on the wild side, and have

00:26:50.899 --> 00:26:52.299
ways of coming back.

00:26:52.299 --> 00:27:02.229
And as soon as the children learn to actually
talk and scribble at the same time--because

00:27:02.229 --> 00:27:08.039
it's not that one appears before the other.
They are, again, it's a bifurcation. When

00:27:08.039 --> 00:27:14.619
the children become two or three years of
age, they acquire what is called the symbolic

00:27:14.619 --> 00:27:21.729
function. It has all these manifestations.
Learning to speak, playing with imaginary

00:27:21.729 --> 00:27:29.239
companions, starting to pretend make- believe,
suspension of disbelief, first sense of humor,

00:27:29.239 --> 00:27:39.299
and an incredible excitement when they can
leave places behind. When they write, and

00:27:39.299 --> 00:27:45.080
when they walk, they become interested in
steps and I think those are very fundamental.

00:27:45.080 --> 00:27:53.889
And they are played out constantly, when the
children are at play, and probably also when

00:27:53.889 --> 00:28:06.649
grown-ups are at play. Why doesn't this one
move now? I had collected quite a few definitions

00:28:06.649 --> 00:28:13.499
of play. but I am going to let them go. There
is one that is important. It's by Winnicott.

00:28:13.499 --> 00:28:22.809
He talks about the importance of transitional
zones, and he talks about these objects that

00:28:22.809 --> 00:28:31.149
very young children fall in love with, because
it in a way, it allows them again to navigate

00:28:31.149 --> 00:28:37.909
the unknown. In this case, it's more relational.
It has to do with the mother that is not always

00:28:37.909 --> 00:28:45.799
so good because every now and then she leaves,
[laughs] and to keep, in a way, this transitional

00:28:45.799 --> 00:28:57.169
object. This blanket, or whatever the object
is, is a way of of actually saying, "I can

00:28:57.169 --> 00:29:03.210
survive. I can survive this separation."

00:29:03.210 --> 00:29:13.139
And they treat the transitional objects in
the way they think they are treated by the

00:29:13.139 --> 00:29:19.210
person who cares about them. So, Winnicott
speaks beautifully about the fact that transitional

00:29:19.210 --> 00:29:27.700
object has to be forgiving enough and resilient
enough to be loved, but also hated. [laughs]

00:29:27.700 --> 00:29:37.039
To be treated in all kinds of ways. And John
Holt is an educator. He says that when children

00:29:37.039 --> 00:29:43.809
play and use fantasies, it's not to get out,
but to get into the world. And this is an

00:29:43.809 --> 00:29:48.309
important idea, because when we see, for example,
kids playing video games, and we spoke about

00:29:48.309 --> 00:29:57.849
this at lunch, it is not that it's an escape.
If I read a book, it's not an escape from

00:29:57.849 --> 00:30:07.640
reality. It gives me the possibility to actually
establish a dialogue between what is and what

00:30:07.640 --> 00:30:15.690
could be. It allows me to invent different
narratives than the ones I am experiencing,

00:30:15.690 --> 00:30:23.549
under certain sets of constraints, by changing
those sets of constraints, and embellishing,

00:30:23.549 --> 00:30:37.369
or the opposite, or just giving, in a way,
the kind of weight and ending that

00:30:37.369 --> 00:30:42.909
feel more desirable.

00:30:42.909 --> 00:30:58.571
Now the play and creativity is another difficult
distinction to make, because it's not the

00:30:58.571 --> 00:31:05.849
same in children in grown-ups, and it's not
necessarily the same in the sciences and in

00:31:05.849 --> 00:31:10.869
the arts. And yet they have something in common.
Many people, in [unintelligible 31:10] in

00:31:10.869 --> 00:31:15.299
particular, they talk about the fact that
creative individuals, scientists, artists,

00:31:15.299 --> 00:31:24.320
or just plain folk usually make better use
of what is called lateral thinking. In a way,

00:31:24.320 --> 00:31:30.349
it's like leaping. An ability to break lose
from acquired mental habits by looking at

00:31:30.349 --> 00:31:39.969
a problem from different angles. Dobono has
been popularized in ways that are not interesting.

00:31:39.969 --> 00:31:50.529
He himself was a deep thinker, [laughter]
and what he says is that lateral thinkers

00:31:50.529 --> 00:31:56.869
tend to explore all of the different ways
of looking at something, rather than accepting

00:31:56.869 --> 00:32:04.639
"This is important," the most promising and
proceeding from that. They do not assume that

00:32:04.639 --> 00:32:13.100
old ideas should have precedence until something
better turns up. This is very Piaget. I mean,

00:32:13.100 --> 00:32:19.059
as long as my world views are good enough,
I'm not going to change.

00:32:19.059 --> 00:32:26.039
So, in a way, it's dominances. It's not that
they are in opposition, but they are people,

00:32:26.039 --> 00:32:32.669
And I notice this with designers a lot, who
are more willing to play that game of not

00:32:32.669 --> 00:32:41.129
taking for granted what actually has become
things they know or believe in. So, one way

00:32:41.129 --> 00:32:59.389
of thinking about this is to say normally,
at least in scientists, the idea is driven

00:32:59.389 --> 00:33:07.081
more by curiosity than playfulness. And those
seem a bit artificial to separate, but I think

00:33:07.081 --> 00:33:13.929
it's a useful thing to do, at least for a
little moment. Curiosity is driven by the

00:33:13.929 --> 00:33:20.429
urge to figure out how things work, to grasp
the logic of the patterns behind the surface

00:33:20.429 --> 00:33:28.889
of things, to explain things away, sometimes
a bit too much. To debug, to prove, and they

00:33:28.889 --> 00:33:34.820
have a clear, adaptive function: to turn the
unfamiliar into the familiar, the unknown

00:33:34.820 --> 00:33:42.909
to the known. Playfulness is doing something
different. It's sort of driven by the desire

00:33:42.909 --> 00:33:48.700
to imagine alternatives, which actually scientists
do a lot if they are innovative. Suspension

00:33:48.700 --> 00:33:57.990
of disbelief is so important. The ability
to fictionalize, to dramatize reality, and

00:33:57.990 --> 00:34:03.559
its adaptive function is likely different
of the one of getting to know. It's to turn

00:34:03.559 --> 00:34:13.750
the familiar, or taken for granted, into the
uncanny, the humorous, the incongruous.

00:34:13.750 --> 00:34:21.419
I am going to pass on this. This is the notion
that it's very important to let the objects

00:34:21.419 --> 00:34:31.760
talk when you are actually in the process
of playing and also in the design process.

00:34:31.760 --> 00:34:41.649
And children at play, or people who are playful
do that a lot. They let the objects talk to

00:34:41.649 --> 00:34:50.409
help them. I am sorry, I am not being clear
on that. There are people who are always able

00:34:50.409 --> 00:34:59.460
to solve problems in ways that allow them
to think less. And those are the real smart

00:34:59.460 --> 00:35:06.740
ones. [laughter] My favorite example is Gaudi
when he was building the models from Sangra

00:35:06.740 --> 00:35:13.970
de Familia. He realized that if he has some
threads hanging, and if he puts weights on

00:35:13.970 --> 00:35:21.110
them, and if you imagine, you put this in
a liquid so you can freeze them, so to speak.

00:35:21.110 --> 00:35:28.599
if you return them, you get the mathematics
of the arches in the other direction. And

00:35:28.599 --> 00:35:34.640
in working with children, I constantly fall
upon children who are good at that. So if

00:35:34.640 --> 00:35:42.420
you are asking a complicated question, like
there is this car driving around the lake,

00:35:42.420 --> 00:35:47.961
and there is this person sitting driving.
He's on the side of the lake when the car

00:35:47.961 --> 00:35:52.050
is on the other side where ... So you have
the kids that go like, "Mmm, mmm." They go

00:35:52.050 --> 00:35:56.970
like this. In this case, it's not using the
object but your own body, instead of looking

00:35:56.970 --> 00:36:01.299
at it from a God's eyes view and calculating
the reverse.

00:36:01.299 --> 00:36:11.039
Or there are children--we were playing a game
of exchanging equal volumes of wood, but they

00:36:11.039 --> 00:36:18.280
were cut in such a vicious way that you had
an equivalence between two blocks against

00:36:18.280 --> 00:36:25.599
three. So, the question was the smallest property
common denominator of the smallest denominator.

00:36:25.599 --> 00:36:33.980
So, the question is, how many blocks does
this person have to give to this person so

00:36:33.980 --> 00:36:38.179
that they have the same amount of wood? You
have these kids that just start somewhere.

00:36:38.179 --> 00:36:47.710
They align the blocks until they align themselves.
And then it's like, "OK." And then they go

00:36:47.710 --> 00:36:54.089
in another direction until it aligns itself
and then guess what, they go in a third direction

00:36:54.089 --> 00:36:59.510
and then they get that cube that is equal
to the other one.

00:36:59.510 --> 00:37:07.940
Never they had counted. Never they had made
a multiplication. And I can multiply the examples

00:37:07.940 --> 00:37:14.250
of working with young children on numbers
where they are incredible. They are able to

00:37:14.250 --> 00:37:20.529
actually reconstruct same quantities elsewhere,
and it's not just perceptive. They would go,

00:37:20.529 --> 00:37:25.210
"And there are three, and there are two, and
there is one, and there is five." So, they

00:37:25.210 --> 00:37:33.069
use the ability to name small quantities perceptually.
They use music as a way to put them in a reason

00:37:33.069 --> 00:37:38.850
to remember. It's like the glue between these
small chunks, and they use this whole thing

00:37:38.850 --> 00:37:44.119
as a recipe to build equivalences at the other
end.

00:37:44.119 --> 00:37:55.049
So, these to me are people who are more at
the end of coming up with creative solutions

00:37:55.049 --> 00:38:03.510
that are clever, in the sense that they are
not based on this rational thinking and this

00:38:03.510 --> 00:38:15.880
power of computation, but in the ability to
establish a dance, really, with the materials,

00:38:15.880 --> 00:38:22.200
and to use so-called sensorimotor abilities
to help the world do half of the work for

00:38:22.200 --> 00:38:24.920
you.

00:38:24.920 --> 00:38:33.980
I think I should finish soon. The future of
play in education, I pass very quickly. The

00:38:33.980 --> 00:38:42.609
problem with play in education--I call it
when the solution becomes the problem. I call

00:38:42.609 --> 00:38:54.630
it, don't kill the play by adding the learning.
Play, by definition, cannot be co- modified,

00:38:54.630 --> 00:39:00.680
and this is the difficulty. So, there is much
talk, especially these days, about measurable

00:39:00.680 --> 00:39:07.650
gains, academic and professional, associated
with play and learning. The problem is to

00:39:07.650 --> 00:39:14.440
me that the slow infusion of ever stronger
doses of serious message to play can actually,

00:39:14.440 --> 00:39:22.060
at once, kill the play and hamper the learning.
In other words, the examples of canned play

00:39:22.060 --> 00:39:28.000
are common in the field of education and also
of "edutainment." It's horrible, but when

00:39:28.000 --> 00:39:39.250
you talk with educators who want to deliver
in more playful ways the contents of the particular

00:39:39.250 --> 00:39:45.970
curriculum and you receive these boxes, it's
like Corn Flakes. Give it to your kids and

00:39:45.970 --> 00:39:56.309
they will become smart. And it has actually
led to scandals with Mozart. Finally, people

00:39:56.309 --> 00:40:06.200
became tired of this rhetoric, of selling
toys, also. I mean, I don't have to go into

00:40:06.200 --> 00:40:08.609
detail more about that.

00:40:08.609 --> 00:40:15.490
The future of play is the idea that today's
children don't always play, learn, or mingle

00:40:15.490 --> 00:40:21.109
the way we expect them to, and what if they
did it in ritual ways that we don't always

00:40:21.109 --> 00:40:28.750
see. There are a few areas of change that
are very clear, even if one doesn't want to

00:40:28.750 --> 00:40:39.160
exaggerate the generational gaps. Today's
children are developing new ways of relating,

00:40:39.160 --> 00:40:46.270
because their neighborhoods, their community
as we know them are no longer the same. So,

00:40:46.270 --> 00:40:52.609
they invent other ways to keep relational
bonds beyond borders.

00:40:52.609 --> 00:41:01.380
Their identities are also shifting, because
in a way, between private and public, the

00:41:01.380 --> 00:41:10.009
boundaries are changing. Also, different ways
of moving between places and what you consider

00:41:10.009 --> 00:41:16.299
home. There are many children who want, if
they have split families, they want either

00:41:16.299 --> 00:41:26.200
to have the same toys in both homes, or they
carry Ninja Turtle bags that are always heavier

00:41:26.200 --> 00:41:32.359
because they actually want to carry the thing
with them. And what they want is they become

00:41:32.359 --> 00:41:38.910
fascinated with all the play that actually
allows them to glide between these places.

00:41:38.910 --> 00:41:47.390
One important difference is also the relationship
to literacy. Because with the tools that we

00:41:47.390 --> 00:41:53.680
have now, even if you think of writing, you
don't have the separation between writing

00:41:53.680 --> 00:42:01.450
and reading anymore. It takes you an evening
to read the book and a year to write it. Now,

00:42:01.450 --> 00:42:08.789
they meet in between, in what I call annotation,
because the reading becomes more and more

00:42:08.789 --> 00:42:14.240
active, because you can cut and paste, you
can highlight, you can re-narrate whatever

00:42:14.240 --> 00:42:21.080
you borrow and read, and you can also, when
you are on the writing side, you take these

00:42:21.080 --> 00:42:27.569
chunks and you sort of pass them on more quick,
quicker. So, you have this circulation and

00:42:27.569 --> 00:42:33.200
distribution of half-baked ideas that is very
characteristic of the way in which kids work

00:42:33.200 --> 00:42:36.010
and that create incredible problems in education.

00:42:36.010 --> 00:42:41.980
But the point that I want to make is that
all this is true. But maybe one of the most

00:42:41.980 --> 00:42:50.869
interesting things is that the children today
are reclaiming their body and reclaiming a

00:42:50.869 --> 00:42:59.970
sense of place. It's as if they have been
in the virtual world for a bit long, so now

00:42:59.970 --> 00:43:05.780
you have the emergence of all these toys like
Wii, but you have also the children who actually

00:43:05.780 --> 00:43:15.950
begin to re-enjoy, even if they go on the
web, they pay more attention to the context,

00:43:15.950 --> 00:43:18.990
the physical context in which they do that.

00:43:18.990 --> 00:43:25.640
So I have one slide here that is sort of the
manifest of the digital kid, what they would

00:43:25.640 --> 00:43:31.460
like. It says, "Give me toys that I can carry
along. Give me toys that make me feel home

00:43:31.460 --> 00:43:38.200
wherever I am. Give me toys that help me find
my ways and orient myself." So, this is besides

00:43:38.200 --> 00:43:47.150
the leaping. "Give me building elements that
I won't lose." This is difficult. If you are

00:43:47.150 --> 00:43:58.980
a builder on the go, Lego won't work because
you lose them. And to capture this notion,

00:43:58.980 --> 00:44:06.271
it's like, "Make me able to explore and use
my creative skills locally, globally, anywhere,

00:44:06.271 --> 00:44:12.660
anyyime but please don't forget, I do have
a body and I like to use it. I am exuberant,

00:44:12.660 --> 00:44:18.460
I'm physical, so make me fly, transport me
and teleport me, but also make me touch, feel,

00:44:18.460 --> 00:44:25.900
move, ground me."

00:44:25.900 --> 00:44:31.769
I think I have to stop. I am going to pass,
because I had, of course, a whole part on

00:44:31.769 --> 00:44:42.089
how you design environments for people to
actually be able to engage in play. One important

00:44:42.089 --> 00:44:47.930
characteristic is that it's good if environments
or artifacts have holding power. This is the

00:44:47.930 --> 00:44:53.140
notion that they just don't say, "Use you,"
wow, and then you get tired of them, but you

00:44:53.140 --> 00:45:00.980
can revisit them constantly and it's actually
growing. "I grow with my toys and my toys

00:45:00.980 --> 00:45:04.650
grow with me" is the idea here.

00:45:04.650 --> 00:45:09.530
There are other qualities of these environments
that are important, that they allow you to

00:45:09.530 --> 00:45:15.640
immerse yourself but then to come back. That
they are forgiving in the sense that nothing

00:45:15.640 --> 00:45:23.630
kills. That's what's so useful in video games.
You can change perspective, you can change

00:45:23.630 --> 00:45:29.019
the scale of things, you become a giant and
then you become a little ant. You can change

00:45:29.019 --> 00:45:40.360
your stance in the world. The notion that
these spaces have always to be comfortable

00:45:40.360 --> 00:45:47.289
in a way. They are often called third places.
They are neither like home nor like work.

00:45:47.289 --> 00:45:53.020
They are more like stages, or cafes, or places
that you know when you come in that you can

00:45:53.020 --> 00:45:58.599
behave differently. It's like, if I go to
a bar, I know I put my red lipstick and so

00:45:58.599 --> 00:46:04.660
on. [laughter] Because it's a third place,
and I can stretch and explore aspects of myself

00:46:04.660 --> 00:46:10.259
that I don't do if I am in a professional
meeting, with some corporate people or something

00:46:10.259 --> 00:46:12.039
like this.

00:46:12.039 --> 00:46:17.759
Another important thing, and I'm so happy
I heard about this here-- it's no segregation

00:46:17.759 --> 00:46:23.230
by age. I had the chance to work for three
years in a European project which had the

00:46:23.230 --> 00:46:29.400
ugly title of "Intergenerational Learning,"
but it's key, it's so important. And that's

00:46:29.400 --> 00:46:34.560
what I like about museums. Because I am a
developmental psychologist, I am always asked,

00:46:34.560 --> 00:46:40.350
when I work mostly with little kids, it's
like, "Is this developmentally correct, is

00:46:40.350 --> 00:46:46.980
this the exact target group for this activity?"
And I'm going, "I am trying to do that well."

00:46:46.980 --> 00:46:51.420
It's like when you are an architect it has
to function but then something else has to

00:46:51.420 --> 00:46:52.420
happen.

00:46:52.420 --> 00:46:58.809
So yes, they have to be targeted. But guess
what? They have to transcend. And in order

00:46:58.809 --> 00:47:06.099
to transcend, they have to please people from
different backgrounds, walks of life, experience,

00:47:06.099 --> 00:47:13.450
age, and they have to have an integrity that
actually engages people from different walks

00:47:13.450 --> 00:47:14.619
of life.

00:47:14.619 --> 00:47:19.650
So in a way, it's to think about places in
which people that otherwise wouldn't meet

00:47:19.650 --> 00:47:24.950
come together to do things that they otherwise
wouldn't do. So, that's a nice definition

00:47:24.950 --> 00:47:29.259
of the types of third places.

00:47:29.259 --> 00:47:46.400
Last slide--if you think about the different
phases in a learning or in a play process,

00:47:46.400 --> 00:47:51.920
sometimes we call it the five C's, it's like,
you have to connect, you have to be inspired,

00:47:51.920 --> 00:47:59.839
it's a wow moment you psych up. And then to
think about the type of space, but not as

00:47:59.839 --> 00:48:05.950
in a physical space that is dedicated, but
the kind of mental space that you need in

00:48:05.950 --> 00:48:16.180
order to be inspired to connect it from. I
call it the imaginarium. If you are immersing

00:48:16.180 --> 00:48:21.599
yourself hands-on, but also body immersion,
if you build, if you try out things, the idea

00:48:21.599 --> 00:48:28.819
is more like the exploratorium. If you think
about things, contemplate, reflect, sometimes

00:48:28.819 --> 00:48:35.859
they call it heads-in, which is an ugly term,
it's more like the sanctuary, because you

00:48:35.859 --> 00:48:42.349
want, in a way, some quiet, in order to play
back. And that's what I spoke the whole evening,

00:48:42.349 --> 00:48:48.261
it's that category, to play back things, to
re- visit, to recast, to give them form to

00:48:48.261 --> 00:49:00.349
ideas, to dramatize, it's the stage. And share,
negotiate, collaborate, mingle, it's the piazza.

00:49:00.349 --> 00:49:08.670
So, these are again, they are--wha-la, I am
done. Thank you.

00:49:08.670 --> 00:49:17.680
I think nothing is harder than to give recommendations
that are specific enough for people who want

00:49:17.680 --> 00:49:22.910
to design environments that are rich for play
and learning. So, I am only scratching the

00:49:22.910 --> 00:49:30.000
surface, because the recommendations are much
too general. And even if you really start

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:35.410
really defining the qualities of these environments,
like they have to allow iteration, they have

00:49:35.410 --> 00:49:40.249
to be forgiving, they have to be good enough
mothers, they have to be this, they have to

00:49:40.249 --> 00:49:48.789
be that, it's always a little bit too general
and it doesn't quite help. I leave this for

00:49:48.789 --> 00:49:59.250
discussion. Thank you. [applause]

00:49:59.250 --> 00:50:12.309
Man 1: Thank you very much. We have a four
year old granddaughter and she likes to play

00:50:12.309 --> 00:50:19.380
hide-and-go-seek and I was wondering, what's
the attraction, or the interest, what it is

00:50:19.380 --> 00:50:20.380
it about hide-and-go-seek?

00:50:20.380 --> 00:50:26.170
Edith: Yes, thank you for this question. [group
laughter] It's starts in babyhood with peek-a-boo.

00:50:26.170 --> 00:50:39.180
It takes various forms. Hide-and-seek is one
form. It all has to do with enactments the

00:50:39.180 --> 00:50:46.520
children enjoy and in which they play a very
active role to engage in to actually explore

00:50:46.520 --> 00:50:57.319
this notion of permanency, object permanency.
When we talk about object permanancy, we don't

00:50:57.319 --> 00:51:06.160
think just of objects, but of people. It starts
with very young children who love these games

00:51:06.160 --> 00:51:11.359
of "I am here, I am not here, I'm here, I'm
not here," the little Debbie that comes out

00:51:11.359 --> 00:51:18.180
of the box, oops, he comes up, he goes back,
it disappears, it comes back because young

00:51:18.180 --> 00:51:23.900
children don't have object permanency. So,
when the ball disappears behind the sofa,

00:51:23.900 --> 00:51:31.950
it's gone. No problem, no attachment. I'm
happy; I'm seduced by the next thing that

00:51:31.950 --> 00:51:33.880
comes my way.

00:51:33.880 --> 00:51:42.800
Around the age of eight months, they begin
to be shy of strangers precisely, because

00:51:42.800 --> 00:51:53.319
there is memory of something that has gone.
It starts developing. And all the games of

00:51:53.319 --> 00:52:02.920
hide-and-seek and so on are different versions
for people of different ages to play out as

00:52:02.920 --> 00:52:14.670
in theater. This coming and going of things,
again it's the absence and the presence, the

00:52:14.670 --> 00:52:15.710
leap, and I come back.

00:52:15.710 --> 00:52:24.420
The hide-and-seek is a little bit particular,
because little kids don't understand what

00:52:24.420 --> 00:52:33.710
they need to do to be really hidden from somebody
else. It's another very interesting thing

00:52:33.710 --> 00:52:45.029
that gets enacted. It's about learning to
take on perspectives, to understand what's

00:52:45.029 --> 00:52:54.640
in the mind of somebody else who's not in
your position. You see? It's one way of actually

00:52:54.640 --> 00:53:01.970
enacting these kinds of permanence about.
But when they are little, they don't get it.

00:53:01.970 --> 00:53:11.690
I don't know about your four year old. I don't
know about how she plays hide-and -seek.

00:53:11.690 --> 00:53:18.490
Man 1: She hides in trees, she hides in the
closet.

00:53:18.490 --> 00:53:24.170
Edith: OK, that's pretty good. [laughter]
It's hard to interpret, but this can almost

00:53:24.170 --> 00:53:35.460
be an enactment of "I know you're going to
find me again." Because there is always this

00:53:35.460 --> 00:53:43.369
double joy and pleasure about "I want to go,
I want to be autonomous, I want separation,

00:53:43.369 --> 00:53:50.930
I want to be Ulysses, I want to be the traveler,
I want not to be attached, but at the same

00:53:50.930 --> 00:53:57.349
time I want to be attached, I want to be pent
up, I want to come back, I want to be comforted."

00:53:57.349 --> 00:54:05.630
And these are very difficult balances to achieve,
and it's at the heart and core of what it

00:54:05.630 --> 00:54:18.569
means to grow as a person, at a personal level
as well as at the cognitive level, or relational

00:54:18.569 --> 00:54:19.569
level.

00:54:19.569 --> 00:54:38.589
These are almost archetypes, and I think that
in the types of obsessions, that the digital

00:54:38.589 --> 00:54:46.099
natives people today engage in have even more
to do with that because they are sometimes

00:54:46.099 --> 00:54:55.849
unwillingly living lives where they constantly
move between worlds. They are more like nomads.

00:54:55.849 --> 00:55:01.109
And what do the nomads do? They always come
back. They have this ritual of coming back.

00:55:01.109 --> 00:55:10.869
So you have many, many examples in a way of
kids. The way I imagine this mythology is

00:55:10.869 --> 00:55:18.619
again about, "I am going; you don't know where
I am. but I am having fun and I am capturing

00:55:18.619 --> 00:55:22.269
things in the environment." It can be with
your cell phone. It's like photographing what

00:55:22.269 --> 00:55:29.230
you eat, you know. "I am going, but I keep
track, I keep trace," it's not even leaving

00:55:29.230 --> 00:55:34.109
a mark but I keep track.

00:55:34.109 --> 00:55:41.989
And then at night, what do I do? I contact
my friends to show them what I have been doing

00:55:41.989 --> 00:55:45.710
because that is as important.

00:55:45.710 --> 00:55:54.200
So in a way, you can all of a sudden shed
light. Some of the technologies that they

00:55:54.200 --> 00:55:58.470
like to carry around or the things that they
like to do through this lens, because you

00:55:58.470 --> 00:56:05.650
begin to realize that they love to share their
captures. They save them for later and then

00:56:05.650 --> 00:56:13.099
they love to share them on common. Even if
it's online, it allows them to play these

00:56:13.099 --> 00:56:24.130
games of "I go, I come back." It's a long
answer to hide and seek but...

00:56:24.130 --> 00:56:27.140
[laughter]

00:56:27.140 --> 00:56:40.820
Woman 1: You were speaking about all the elements,
in a public space of inspiring kids to be

00:56:40.820 --> 00:56:47.660
hands on and to immerse themselves. But, now
we have a society in a lot of public spaces

00:56:47.660 --> 00:56:50.400
where everybody has hand sanitizer. [laughter]

00:56:50.400 --> 00:56:51.400
Edith: Yes.

00:56:51.400 --> 00:56:55.711
Woman 1: And they don't want kids to touch
anything. I work in a hospital so...

00:56:55.711 --> 00:56:56.711
Edith: Yes.

00:56:56.711 --> 00:56:57.711
Woman 1: That challenge of inspire...

00:56:57.711 --> 00:56:58.711
Edith: I know.

00:56:58.711 --> 00:57:05.769
Woman 1: ...get the kids to move, be a part
of something, but don't touch it really. Could

00:57:05.769 --> 00:57:06.769
you comment on that?

00:57:06.769 --> 00:57:19.369
Edith: I have no good answer. I only have
hopes that I see symptoms of

00:57:19.369 --> 00:57:29.980
new rituals around that we see that go counter
this tendency. And the example I have in mind

00:57:29.980 --> 00:57:36.730
is the maker's fair. It's a very strange phenomenon
where all of a sudden, you have all these

00:57:36.730 --> 00:57:42.970
people who have lived online, who have created
their own second lives and all these things.

00:57:42.970 --> 00:57:50.039
They come together, like, to go to Woodstock
you know? In tribes. And what do they do?

00:57:50.039 --> 00:58:01.530
They bring stuff and they show it to one another
and it has to be in a physical place and some

00:58:01.530 --> 00:58:08.710
people like it, some people don't. Some people
think its not really arts, it's not a novelty.

00:58:08.710 --> 00:58:19.859
But I cannot answer your question. I can just
try to become attentive while this trend is

00:58:19.859 --> 00:58:33.490
certainly very strong of creative ways in
which younger people actually deal with these

00:58:33.490 --> 00:58:38.930
kinds of challenges. And one of them was also
that there was an incredible resilience at

00:58:38.930 --> 00:58:46.000
a certain point on the part of the geeks,
the ones that live constantly in the virtual

00:58:46.000 --> 00:58:49.749
world, to actually establish rapport with
other people that are not physical.

00:58:49.749 --> 00:58:58.380
They were very close, but it was not necessarily
physically close. And I don't know what this

00:58:58.380 --> 00:59:10.799
does. On the other hand, you have these ways
in which people come together, also in hybrid

00:59:10.799 --> 00:59:12.750
ways that are very interesting.

00:59:12.750 --> 00:59:23.530
It's not just only online. And I become myself
more and more interested in studying, not

00:59:23.530 --> 00:59:33.019
just when you look at kids or young people
staring at the computer screen and entering

00:59:33.019 --> 00:59:40.259
their world, it's interesting to look at how
they actually go in each other's houses.

00:59:40.259 --> 00:59:47.619
There are lots of rituals around this that
go beyond just what they stare at. They like

00:59:47.619 --> 00:59:57.589
to slouch on couches, they have their own
way of keeping a physical rapport with the

00:59:57.589 --> 01:00:06.540
place and other people, even if they go away
ever now and then. It's like when we have

01:00:06.540 --> 01:00:09.960
our cell phones, we're still here.

01:00:09.960 --> 01:00:20.599
I think the only way to go about this is to
study these hybrids where physical, virtual,

01:00:20.599 --> 01:00:25.960
and digital merge in interesting and unusual
ways. That's all I can say.

01:00:25.960 --> 01:00:34.269
It's very difficult, too. But I agree, I arrived
yesterday in Minneapolis at three o'clock,

01:00:34.269 --> 01:00:43.940
and I am in the at the center of the city.
The financial center, I guess. And my question

01:00:43.940 --> 01:00:48.760
was "Donde esta la gente? Where are the people
here?" [laughter]

01:00:48.760 --> 01:00:54.740
You have these streets that are totally empty,
you have these gorgeous huge buildings, the

01:00:54.740 --> 01:01:02.589
windows of which you cannot open most of the
time. It is a striking shock when you arrive.

01:01:02.589 --> 01:01:13.210
But I am an optimist, because I think that
people are very smart in inventing for themselves

01:01:13.210 --> 01:01:23.599
places, and environments, and rituals that
are made for that.

01:01:23.599 --> 01:01:29.230
I come from Europe and I was an intellectual
in the 60's, and the cafe was sacred, and

01:01:29.230 --> 01:01:36.130
so on. But I enjoy when I see the all the
geeks working at their own computer in the

01:01:36.130 --> 01:01:37.140
cyber cafe.

01:01:37.140 --> 01:01:42.749
It's not the same, but it's a funny way that
they have to come together without talking

01:01:42.749 --> 01:01:47.829
to one another. [laughter]

01:01:47.829 --> 01:01:57.079
Now they start doing things together. They
communicate a lot through what they are doing,

01:01:57.079 --> 01:02:15.900
which is also can lead to some--and they jump.
What is also very nice is that at the Exploratorium--I

01:02:15.900 --> 01:02:27.430
don't know if you were there, Keith--at the
Exploratorium. They did a whole activity around

01:02:27.430 --> 01:02:39.039
sound. There was actually one session where
very known artists, sound artists, came to

01:02:39.039 --> 01:02:47.420
talk about their work. And it was very nice
to see the kinds of questions from the young

01:02:47.420 --> 01:02:58.500
makers. They had no sense of hierarchy, they
had no sense of comparing their production

01:02:58.500 --> 01:03:04.950
with the production of Walter Ketundu or other
wonderful artists who work with sound.

01:03:04.950 --> 01:03:17.040
The questions were all technical. "How do
you get this sound to come out from these

01:03:17.040 --> 01:03:23.480
tubes?" The questions were all technical.
And I found this very interesting because

01:03:23.480 --> 01:03:35.960
it's as if they were in this world of hobbyists,
of makers that work together and the question

01:03:35.960 --> 01:03:45.030
is, "How to optimize what I am really interested
in doing?" So, they are very refreshing in

01:03:45.030 --> 01:03:52.779
the questions they ask, because it's not like
the usual shyness, you know in front of the

01:03:52.779 --> 01:03:53.779
big artist.

01:03:53.779 --> 01:04:01.009
I don't know what it entails in terms of the
quality of their work. It's not enough to

01:04:01.009 --> 01:04:07.769
just throw out something and then it's enough.
And when I have organized workshops for kids'

01:04:07.769 --> 01:04:14.920
designers, kids' play, kids' technology, more
than you ever want to participate in any more

01:04:14.920 --> 01:04:21.130
and usually they stop when every kid comes
with a little contraption they have built.

01:04:21.130 --> 01:04:24.979
It's more or less cute or not. You can say,
"Yeah, it's great."

01:04:24.979 --> 01:04:33.170
But you never have the time to come back and
then actually either really respect the work

01:04:33.170 --> 01:04:39.650
by re-choreographing it in different ways
or imagining games like, the children come

01:04:39.650 --> 01:04:44.589
back with what they have produced and you
go for a second round, saying for example,

01:04:44.589 --> 01:04:49.380
"Take whichever inspires you best, even if
it's not your own and continue to work on

01:04:49.380 --> 01:04:58.420
it as if it were your own." And you go through
alterations, and there has to be way to allow

01:04:58.420 --> 01:05:05.569
these productions to become better crafted,
to become more polished and also to become

01:05:05.569 --> 01:05:11.730
something that is bigger than just my own
little first attempt at sketching something.

01:05:11.730 --> 01:05:16.950
This is very important, but it's easier said
than done.

01:05:16.950 --> 01:05:27.549
Man 2: I have a question for you. You've been
talking about the internal, technical inside

01:05:27.549 --> 01:05:35.970
space lifestyles of children. And in this
country, we're becoming more urbanized. What

01:05:35.970 --> 01:05:42.059
do you perceive are changes taking place in
how families raise their children in more

01:05:42.059 --> 01:05:45.999
urban environments versus say forty years
ago...

01:05:45.999 --> 01:05:46.999
Edith: Yes.

01:05:46.999 --> 01:05:49.450
Man 2: ...when it was more suburban and rural?

01:05:49.450 --> 01:06:04.359
Edith: Yes. That's a whole other, very complicated
question. [laughter] I like to almost start

01:06:04.359 --> 01:06:10.079
from the point of view of the child, thinking
sort of what's in their mind, what's on their

01:06:10.079 --> 01:06:19.160
plate and what's in their toy chests, you
know? And from the point of view of the child,

01:06:19.160 --> 01:06:31.759
oftentimes they have single parents or they
have split family situations. They also have

01:06:31.759 --> 01:06:39.940
situations where parents get remarried after
having had children so there are many kids

01:06:39.940 --> 01:06:45.119
around, like it's this tribe of kids. So,
I always like to think that the kids are thinking,

01:06:45.119 --> 01:06:51.970
"I love to go out with my family, but can
you tell me who is part of my family?" [laughter]

01:06:51.970 --> 01:06:58.559
And in a way, they have their own way of--because
it's not so clear sometimes--they have their

01:06:58.559 --> 01:07:08.450
own way of reinventing the family in terms
of the kinds of people that they want around

01:07:08.450 --> 01:07:14.890
themselves to feel good. Those are all the
examples. We worked a lot on this one at Lego

01:07:14.890 --> 01:07:21.210
to try to understand how they respond to these
situations precisely.

01:07:21.210 --> 01:07:33.829
For example, if you live in one apartment
in town, at some point in another, the kids

01:07:33.829 --> 01:07:46.930
are very different. Either they like to have
duplicates, to feel at home almost anywhere

01:07:46.930 --> 01:07:48.239
which is not really home.

01:07:48.239 --> 01:07:57.059
It's like in America, when you travel. I think
Americans are a nomadic tribe without them

01:07:57.059 --> 01:08:04.031
realizing it because they came to this country
and they were more pioneers. So, this nice

01:08:04.031 --> 01:08:13.749
thing--it's not just the mobile home, it's
the thing that you find wherever you go. It

01:08:13.749 --> 01:08:20.569
can be the McDonald's. When you go to a park,
it's that particular bench.

01:08:20.569 --> 01:08:27.580
So, when people come, in a way, it is the
same because it's a duplicate of it. So, if

01:08:27.580 --> 01:08:33.750
a child travels with their parent and each
time when you stop you find those benches,

01:08:33.750 --> 01:08:40.529
it's OK and you eat your McDonald's. It's
OK. It's not too disturbing.

01:08:40.529 --> 01:08:49.359
So, that's for the part of being comforted.
It's the notion of the carpet that the nomadic

01:08:49.359 --> 01:08:53.900
people carry with them, and that is the most
precious object because wherever they go they

01:08:53.900 --> 01:08:58.779
stake their territory, they sit on it, and
they are home.

01:08:58.779 --> 01:09:04.600
And there are all these nice studies that
are done also, by artists, about how people

01:09:04.600 --> 01:09:13.650
inhabit spaces that they know they cannot
stay long in, like hotels, or how they relate

01:09:13.650 --> 01:09:19.580
to their luggage, to their objects in luggage.
Again, there are very different strategies.

01:09:19.580 --> 01:09:26.819
There are those who, no matter where they
are in the hotel, how horrible or beautiful,

01:09:26.819 --> 01:09:32.940
they always have to put a few objects in a
very particular place to stake their territory

01:09:32.940 --> 01:09:34.509
and feel at home.

01:09:34.509 --> 01:09:38.739
Others don't care. They put themselves in
a bubble. The only thing they want is the

01:09:38.739 --> 01:09:46.659
plug to put on their computer, because that's
home. I go through my email, I am comforted.

01:09:46.659 --> 01:09:55.699
And a shower should be good, and the bed should
be good. They stay in a bubble and that's

01:09:55.699 --> 01:09:58.239
what they bring with themselves.

01:09:58.239 --> 01:10:05.060
The children do that a lot, for example, in
the world of toys. I've worked for Lego for

01:10:05.060 --> 01:10:11.640
so long. At the beginning it was all about
bricks but you can't carry bricks along, so

01:10:11.640 --> 01:10:18.110
if you have different places where you go
to, you just need your stack of bricks. If

01:10:18.110 --> 01:10:24.840
children travel, what they like is to bring
little micro-worlds with themselves, the little

01:10:24.840 --> 01:10:30.701
mini-worlds where you can do things, or the
video games because they come with you.

01:10:30.701 --> 01:10:39.060
I understand that they like to bring their
little--I like to bring my computer with me

01:10:39.060 --> 01:10:50.290
now when I travel because again, it's a way
of settling. It's about settling even if you

01:10:50.290 --> 01:10:58.370
know that the places in which you settle are
totally not permanent and fragile.

01:10:58.370 --> 01:11:06.090
Man 3: You talked about perspective in play.
Can you talk a little bit about, especially

01:11:06.090 --> 01:11:14.940
for the digital natives, about watching themselves
play, that phenomena of them being on camera

01:11:14.940 --> 01:11:16.030
or them seeing themselves?

01:11:16.030 --> 01:11:23.010
Edith: Can you say a bit more what they do?

01:11:23.010 --> 01:11:28.110
Man 3: Sure. Well, it came mostly from another
generation of the MTV generation, where they

01:11:28.110 --> 01:11:34.781
watched themselves dance or do things, and
their behavior changed. Now, with some of

01:11:34.781 --> 01:11:40.111
these digital natives, with virtual cam, webcam,
they're watching themselves behave in a way

01:11:40.111 --> 01:11:44.760
and they're changing. And also Skype. They're
watching themselves change how they play.

01:11:44.760 --> 01:11:59.180
Edith: Yes, this is wonderful, because it
is almost a technique to allow to modify as

01:11:59.180 --> 01:12:09.310
you go along the narrative, and also the way
in which you want to portray yourself, because

01:12:09.310 --> 01:12:15.489
it keeps track of what you are doing in a
way that you cannot appreciate if you are

01:12:15.489 --> 01:12:29.530
really in the actions. So, it's a way of actually
having these mirrors. Mirrors have been important,

01:12:29.530 --> 01:12:35.530
like in the elementary school system for young
children, they use mirrors a lot, and all

01:12:35.530 --> 01:12:40.491
kinds of mirrors. They have, for example,
these triangular mirrors where people go in;

01:12:40.491 --> 01:12:57.070
they have all kinds of mirrors, and maybe
it's a way to keep track and to be able to

01:12:57.070 --> 01:13:08.380
operate these desired changes in their own
appearance, identity, to play around to explore

01:13:08.380 --> 01:13:17.420
that. I don't quite know. What strikes me
also is all these versions of karaoke. I don't

01:13:17.420 --> 01:13:27.110
know what this about. [laughter] I am sure
you have better insights than I do on this

01:13:27.110 --> 01:13:28.270
question. [laughter]

01:13:28.270 --> 01:13:31.940
Man 4: Why do we like Legos so much?

01:13:31.940 --> 01:13:41.030
Edith: My goodness. OK. This has to do, I
think, with I said at some point that one

01:13:41.030 --> 01:13:49.110
quality of the environment can be "I grow
with my toys, my toys grow with me." And they

01:13:49.110 --> 01:13:55.800
are very constrained, and I think they have
affordances that don't please everybody. So,

01:13:55.800 --> 01:14:04.380
what they are good at is, some people call
it, "low-floor, high-ceiling." You enter,

01:14:04.380 --> 01:14:10.810
you begin to use your hands, even if you have
no ideas, you build something and it gives

01:14:10.810 --> 01:14:16.740
you an idea. And then you change it, and it
gives you an idea. So, it's this notion that

01:14:16.740 --> 01:14:24.510
you start by meddling around, even if you
have no ideas, and it's delightful to see

01:14:24.510 --> 01:14:31.659
that as you go along, you see things that
are surprising, and it makes you have better

01:14:31.659 --> 01:14:38.300
ideas, and you go on, and you sharpen it.
It's not like solving a problem that has one

01:14:38.300 --> 01:14:44.400
definite solution. So, that's one thing that
I think we like. What we like is the modular

01:14:44.400 --> 01:14:53.880
quality of the environment. You know somehow
that there are many different ways of connecting

01:14:53.880 --> 01:15:00.780
things, and that it will always work, because
the system is modular, even if it's very constrained.

01:15:00.780 --> 01:15:09.199
I think also that the rapport itself has become
rich enough that you can move beyond just

01:15:09.199 --> 01:15:15.670
building towers and orthogonal structures.
You can start doing other things. If you add

01:15:15.670 --> 01:15:20.150
motors or sensors, you can start doing interesting
things.

01:15:20.150 --> 01:15:29.360
What I think Lego does not address is the
"wide walls" aspect. When people talk about

01:15:29.360 --> 01:15:36.380
"low-floor, high-ceiling, " there is this
notion of "wide walls." It attracts many different

01:15:36.380 --> 01:15:45.560
types of people, because it is very layered,
and it is very rich in the sense that it speaks

01:15:45.560 --> 01:15:56.030
to many people. And I think Lego, in a way,
speaks to boys. It wants to speak to boys.

01:15:56.030 --> 01:16:03.099
It doesn't mean that many girls are not interested
in it, but they have been working along this

01:16:03.099 --> 01:16:10.650
path of just developing these wonderful elements
to build these organized structures.

01:16:10.650 --> 01:16:16.510
But what you see in workshops is that there
are some people who are more attracted to

01:16:16.510 --> 01:16:24.949
using--let's say, even a contraption that
does something, like a robot or a conveyor

01:16:24.949 --> 01:16:28.820
belt or some mechanical device.

01:16:28.820 --> 01:16:36.119
They come from a world in which they like
more--I call it an organic rather than mechanical

01:16:36.119 --> 01:16:45.170
metaphors--that they live by. And they like
to create little creatures that will sense

01:16:45.170 --> 01:16:53.261
if there is no more water in the Earth for
the plant and that will just water the plant.

01:16:53.261 --> 01:16:58.600
Or some device that does something in the
environment, but it's not you that control

01:16:58.600 --> 01:17:02.280
it or it controls something else, in very
mechanical terms.

01:17:02.280 --> 01:17:11.420
So, in a way, Lego, I think, they have tried.
They actually, at some point, they were working

01:17:11.420 --> 01:17:20.960
on a very interesting system--what was it
called? I forgot the name--where you could

01:17:20.960 --> 01:17:23.619
do jewelry in these modular ways.

01:17:23.619 --> 01:17:30.360
And they had watches where you could do them
in a modular way. Snap it or it had a word

01:17:30.360 --> 01:17:39.489
like this. Snap it together. They were wonderful.
They were like plexi-wires, and you had these

01:17:39.489 --> 01:17:44.690
elements that you could click on it so you
could do colors, you could do earrings.

01:17:44.690 --> 01:17:53.310
But you could also, if you are a little boy,
you could do accessories. You could do decoration

01:17:53.310 --> 01:17:58.860
because you could attach them on other objects
in the environment. They had a wonderful quality,

01:17:58.860 --> 01:18:10.940
but they never made it because I think the
culture, itself, is growing around the brick.

01:18:10.940 --> 01:18:14.280
And it's the brick.

01:18:14.280 --> 01:18:16.760
[laughter]

01:18:16.760 --> 01:18:27.989
Woman 2: It was mentioned that you have had
some influence from Maria Montessori and I'm

01:18:27.989 --> 01:18:34.090
just wondering if--as a Montessorian, it's
always interesting to hear what parts people

01:18:34.090 --> 01:18:38.699
are attracted to and I can imagine certain
things, but I'm just wondering if you can

01:18:38.699 --> 01:18:42.530
expand on that a bit.

01:18:42.530 --> 01:18:48.949
Edith: Yeah, there are two things that Madame
Montessori did very well. I think it's this

01:18:48.949 --> 01:18:59.710
notion that if you actually want to open space
in the children's minds, their environment

01:18:59.710 --> 01:19:09.190
has to be pretty well organized. It's this
paradox that a space, especially for little

01:19:09.190 --> 01:19:17.340
children, they have to know where the materials
are, where they can find them, to actually

01:19:17.340 --> 01:19:22.250
help them understand that they could use them.

01:19:22.250 --> 01:19:32.210
If everything is messy around, if there is
absolutely no clarity in what could be found

01:19:32.210 --> 01:19:37.920
where, it makes it more difficult to be creative.
It's a paradoxical idea.

01:19:37.920 --> 01:19:46.349
And from my readings of the text of Maria
Montessori is that--and this comes through

01:19:46.349 --> 01:19:56.820
very much in the original Emelia infant toddler
schools. It is very organized, but not as

01:19:56.820 --> 01:20:00.590
in a military way, in a very gentle way.

01:20:00.590 --> 01:20:06.510
And the children are invited to the rituals
of bringing the things back. And we can play,

01:20:06.510 --> 01:20:12.719
we open it up, but we bring them back so that
everybody knows where things are.

01:20:12.719 --> 01:20:18.780
That's one thing and the other one is just--many
of these materials have the quality of the

01:20:18.780 --> 01:20:29.230
Froebel gifts. They are extraordinary embodiments
of aspects of a phenomenon that you can play

01:20:29.230 --> 01:20:39.489
with. But then again, the danger is if somebody
who is not very knowledgeable about the fact

01:20:39.489 --> 01:20:44.550
that if you want children to understand a
phenomenon you can have too various of those

01:20:44.550 --> 01:20:48.300
embodiments. Maybe it can become too rigid.
I don't know.

01:20:48.300 --> 01:20:53.340
But for example, the Montessori bells. You
could imagine very different ways in which

01:20:53.340 --> 01:21:01.570
you can give a child a sense of music, and
the Montessori bells are just one possible

01:21:01.570 --> 01:21:07.489
embodiment of aspects of the phenomenon. Then
you can have games where, for example, you

01:21:07.489 --> 01:21:14.920
play with the synesthesia between movement
and sound. You could have just playing an

01:21:14.920 --> 01:21:21.360
instrument. You could have building reason
machines.

01:21:21.360 --> 01:21:25.920
Probably she was very good at this. I don't
know enough about Montessori because it's

01:21:25.920 --> 01:21:32.010
not really from within. I have never really
worked in Montessori school, but the art is

01:21:32.010 --> 01:21:40.250
really to imagine these different ways in
which you can unfold the phenomenon, because

01:21:40.250 --> 01:21:43.260
otherwise embodiments are not good enough.

01:21:43.260 --> 01:21:52.469
You see, it makes me sad and sick when I hear
now there is this incredible fad among academics.

01:21:52.469 --> 01:21:59.760
Everything has to be embodied. Embodied cognition.
And it is nothing else than--I call it in

01:21:59.760 --> 01:22:06.310
my own academic term--it's a return of a certain
kind of empiricism. It's like, "Play with

01:22:06.310 --> 01:22:12.550
this thing. Play with these cakes and you
will understand fractions." Or, "Play with

01:22:12.550 --> 01:22:17.079
these materials." Because the idea is that
the concept is embodied in the materials,

01:22:17.079 --> 01:22:19.300
which is never the case.

01:22:19.300 --> 01:22:27.469
But I think the Montessori materials, especially
the way they are used in Montessori schools,

01:22:27.469 --> 01:22:34.190
do that very well. The third thing I like
about her is that the senses are important

01:22:34.190 --> 01:22:40.690
to her, maybe more than to Piaget, because
he was more interested in the child as a young

01:22:40.690 --> 01:22:42.739
scientist and was more heady.

01:22:42.739 --> 01:22:47.579
So, the materials that they came up with,
this game-like situation in which you invite

01:22:47.579 --> 01:22:54.190
the kids to play, they are also kind of nice
but not as nice. He doesn't pay attention

01:22:54.190 --> 01:22:59.740
to the senses so much, which Madame Montessori
does. I don't know if there are still aspects

01:22:59.740 --> 01:23:06.860
that you think of that I let aside that you
enjoy.

01:23:06.860 --> 01:23:22.000
Woman 2: Well it was interesting to hear you
talk about the child as the foreigner. She

01:23:22.000 --> 01:23:29.230
had thought that it was important for the
child to have a heavy dose of realism in her

01:23:29.230 --> 01:23:34.480
stages of development. The realism comes first,
and the imagination later. As a teacher I

01:23:34.480 --> 01:23:40.159
always have trouble talking with parents about
that. They get very upset about me poo-pooing

01:23:40.159 --> 01:23:47.320
Winnie the Pooh, for instance, at least the
Disney version anyway. When in fact, what

01:23:47.320 --> 01:23:56.159
the children in the Montessori tradition are
needing is practical life skills. You talked

01:23:56.159 --> 01:24:05.980
about the sensorial materials that Maria Montessori
created for her environments. That's an important

01:24:05.980 --> 01:24:09.300
difference between, say something like Waldorf.

01:24:09.300 --> 01:24:19.409
Edith: Yes, I think that they solve it very
well. If I think of the schools of Reggio,

01:24:19.409 --> 01:24:29.840
Reggio Emilia--they have this incredible,
beautiful way of working with very young children.

01:24:29.840 --> 01:24:36.920
You can almost think of it as--if Madame Montessori
was still there she would be in that school

01:24:36.920 --> 01:24:42.099
where many artists work with children like
Bruno Monari, or people who work on children

01:24:42.099 --> 01:24:50.831
and poetry and it's very rich. And in a way
it's true. They would say things like "we

01:24:50.831 --> 01:24:57.270
are what we eat" and the children have to
behave in a way, they have these lovely kitchen

01:24:57.270 --> 01:25:04.560
and you see the kids, they are not completely
crazy. And they have these places where they

01:25:04.560 --> 01:25:12.290
actually play games like going to the store,
where they do these things. But at the same

01:25:12.290 --> 01:25:18.100
time they also do these completely different
types of things. They crawl through mirrors,

01:25:18.100 --> 01:25:24.329
they do things like this, but they also do
these installations. The kids do installations.

01:25:24.329 --> 01:25:32.530
So, for example they used the overhead projector
to project things and when you see the children

01:25:32.530 --> 01:25:39.969
play, it's the same. I think us adults, we
have poor ways of establishing distinction

01:25:39.969 --> 01:25:50.270
between realities and fiction. It was a constant
discussion at Lego. And I think it's a better

01:25:50.270 --> 01:26:00.130
way to think of the fact that every reality
is an invented reality, and sometimes you

01:26:00.130 --> 01:26:14.420
need to play out things that seem very mundane.
It's nice to do the dishes when mom does the

01:26:14.420 --> 01:26:21.260
dishes, but sometimes it's also about something
else, you know? Sometimes it's about hide-and-seek,

01:26:21.260 --> 01:26:26.710
because there are not just these pragmatic
things that make us who we are, it's also

01:26:26.710 --> 01:26:35.199
the fears that we have, the way we see ourselves,
the way we relate to other people and they

01:26:35.199 --> 01:26:40.699
are traditions and groupings. Play types are
all about that.

01:26:40.699 --> 01:26:48.370
So, my suggestion would be to try to break
this distinction the way we usually do it,

01:26:48.370 --> 01:26:55.570
between things that are more realistic and
things that are more imaginary. Or even in

01:26:55.570 --> 01:27:00.860
kids' drawings, the realistic phase in the
kids' drawing actually comes later, because

01:27:00.860 --> 01:27:10.219
when they are little they draw what they know,
what they imagine, and then it becomes more

01:27:10.219 --> 01:27:12.170
realistic.

01:27:12.170 --> 01:27:20.489
I don't know, but I myself find it useful
to abandon these dichotomies. Because you

01:27:20.489 --> 01:27:27.400
have too many people who say, "The kids from
inner city, they cannot pretend--for them

01:27:27.400 --> 01:27:35.120
it has to be very pragmatic. The girls, the
girls are more like realistic and the boys..."

01:27:35.120 --> 01:27:42.340
I did lot of studies of young children's pretense.
And the fundamentals are less about that than

01:27:42.340 --> 01:27:44.020
how they engage with play.

01:27:44.020 --> 01:27:49.310
So, I did notice something, it's neither the
girls are controlling when they are in the

01:27:49.310 --> 01:27:56.271
kitchen and the boys when it is outside or
the girls play more realistic. It's more about,

01:27:56.271 --> 01:28:04.440
for the girls, it's like they choreograph.
So they are like a giant and they have this

01:28:04.440 --> 01:28:13.150
little thing, "This one talks to this one,
and this one talks to this one." In a way,

01:28:13.150 --> 01:28:17.810
they take on a God's eye view and everybody
always says the girls are more immersed.

01:28:17.810 --> 01:28:23.639
The boys go, "Bvvvvvvvv, " [laughter] so it's
like their body almost becomes the airplane

01:28:23.639 --> 01:28:30.790
and so on. So, I don't know if it's a boy/girl
thing and I would not want to have me quoted

01:28:30.790 --> 01:28:37.650
on it, but there are these different ways
in which people--and again, it shifts because

01:28:37.650 --> 01:28:43.660
the ones that at some moment are the choreographers
then they dwell in, because they project themselves

01:28:43.660 --> 01:28:48.671
in the character, they make it grow, they
speak from the point of view of the character

01:28:48.671 --> 01:28:58.130
and no longer just organizing. And it's very
difficult to talk about reality or fiction

01:28:58.130 --> 01:29:01.739
in that way because sometimes, I don't know,
reality is the strangest of all fictions sometimes.

01:29:01.739 --> 01:29:02.980
[laughter]

01:29:02.980 --> 01:29:12.139
Woman 2: Thank you very much. [applause]

01:29:12.139 --> 01:29:17.570
Edith: Thank you.

